+### Task 2: API — accept and return `dueDate`
+
+**Files:**
+- Modify: `src/DemoTodo/Program.cs` (only if needed — the minimal API binds `NewTodo`/`TodoPatch` directly, so likely no change)
+- Test: `tests/DemoTodo.Tests/TodoApiTests.cs`
+
+**Interfaces:**
+- Consumes: `NewTodo(string? Title, DateOnly? DueDate)`, `TodoPatch(string? Title, bool? Done, DateOnly? DueDate, bool DueDateSet)` from Task 1.
+- Produces: `POST /todos` accepts `{"title": "...", "dueDate": "2026-10-25"}`; `PATCH /todos/{id}` accepts `{"dueDate": "..."}` to set and `{"dueDate": null}` to clear; responses include `"dueDate":"yyyy-MM-dd"` or `"dueDate":null`.
+
+**JSON binding note:** System.Text.Json cannot distinguish "key absent" from "key null" for a nullable value type by default. To make `PATCH {"dueDate": null}` clear the date while an absent key leaves it unchanged, `TodoPatch` needs `DueDateSet`. With records bound from JSON, the clean way without new packages is: keep `TodoPatch` as the store-level type, and in `Program.cs` bind the PATCH body to a small DTO that uses `JsonElement?` or `JsonNode` to detect key presence, then map to `TodoPatch`. **Simpler alternative that the spec allows:** treat `dueDate: null` in PATCH as "clear" and an absent key as "unchanged" by binding to `JsonNode`/`JsonElement` in the handler. Implement whichever is smallest; the tests below pin the behavior, not the mechanism.
